INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Rows of washers churning. A flickering sign in the window. The smell of detergent and hot lint.

NORMAN BELLWETHER, 50s, in pajama pants and a trench coat, folds a towel with military precision. Two machines over, his daughter, LEOCADIA, 20s, stares at a spinning drum with a duffel bag at her feet.

                    NORMAN
(to himself)
Corners to corners.

                    LEOCADIA
Dad.

                    NORMAN
Mm.

                    LEOCADIA
Why are you here?

                    NORMAN
Your mother said you were.

                    LEOCADIA
That's not why.

                    NORMAN
My machine broke.

                    LEOCADIA
It's two in the morning.

                    NORMAN
I couldn't sleep.

She watches the drum. He folds the towel again, makes a perfect square, sets it on top of a stack.

                    LEOCADIA
You don't have to follow me around.

                    NORMAN
I wasn't.

                    LEOCADIA
You were in the car outside my apartment last night.

He stops folding. Looks at the towel as though it might help him.

                    NORMAN
It was on my way.

                    LEOCADIA
To what?

                    NORMAN
(to the room)
Nowhere. Everywhere.

The machine beeps. Spin cycle ending. Leocadia opens the door, pulls out a damp bundle, and begins to load a dryer. Norman moves to help. She stops him with a look.

                    LEOCADIA
I'm leaving Friday.

                    NORMAN
I know.

                    LEOCADIA
Mom told you?

                    NORMAN
She didn't have to.

He takes a sock off the pile, turns it right side out, hands it to her. She takes it, and he lets his fingers rest against hers a moment too long.

                    LEOCADIA
Dad.

                    NORMAN
I'm fine.

                    LEOCADIA
You're crying.

                    NORMAN
It's the lint.

She laughs through her nose. He turns away, pulls a handkerchief from the trench coat, and blows it with great dignity.

                    NORMAN (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Damn lint.

She loads the rest of the clothes, closes the dryer door, and feeds in quarters one at a time. The drum begins to turn.

                    LEOCADIA
Sit with me.

They sit on the long bench in front of the dryer. The clothes tumble in warm circles.

                    NORMAN
Do you have enough socks?

                    LEOCADIA
Dad.

                    NORMAN
I'm only asking.

Her head tips onto his shoulder. He freezes, then slowly lets his cheek rest against her hair.
